expanded:
First, it should be pointed out that the term “Highest common ancestor” is nonstandard. It is not the usual way that tree-related concepts are described in most standard discussions. Because the phrase is not standard, it is especially important to consider what it might mean before proceeding. If it means the common ancestor that is closest to the root, then the situation becomes much simpler and more direct. In that case, for two existing nodes that are located in one and the same rooted binary tree, the common ancestor closest to the root is simply the root itself. In other words, if “highest” is interpreted as being nearest to the top of the tree, then the root is the node that satisfies that description, because no node can be closer to the root than the root itself. So, under that reading of the phrase, the answer is always the root.

At the same time, the function should also verify membership of both nodes before it decides what to return. This means that, rather than simply assuming the two nodes are present, the implementation should first check whether both a and b are actually contained somewhere in the tree. To accomplish that, a helper function can be used to check for the presence of a given target node within the tree starting from a specified root. The main function can then use that helper to confirm that both nodes exist. If the root exists and both membership checks succeed, the root is returned; if either check fails, the function returns None. The code is shown below:

```python
def contains(root, target):
    return bool(root) and (root is target or contains(root.left,target) or contains(root.right,target))

def highest_common_ancestor(root, a, b):
    return root if root and contains(root,a) and contains(root,b) else None
```

